WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.270
The last British royal wedding -- between
Prince William and Kate Middleton, brought

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:09.180
together 1,900 guests, many of whom were pretty
high profile people.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.610
Footballer David Beckham was there with
his wife and fashion designer Victoria Beckham.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:14.840
Elton John was there too.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:18.579
But this photo might be the most impressive
of all, because

00:00:18.579 --> 00:00:21.659
This is the Queen of Spain
And the Prince of Spain

00:00:21.659 --> 00:00:23.730
Next to the Princess of Sweden,

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.130
the former King of Greece is behind them,

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.080
And way back there is the former King of Romania.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:34.080
They were invited because they’re all related
to Prince William’s great-great-great Grandmother,

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.300
Queen Victoria.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.980
Over the course of her 63-year reign, she
strategically planned marriages to place her

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:42.510
descendants in royal families all over Europe.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:46.760
And in doing so, created one of the most remarkable
royal families in history.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:00.380
By the early 19th century, Europe had been
at war for decades.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:06.140
After the French Revolutionary and Napoleonic
wars killed millions, European leaders came

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.640
together to restore peace by reshaping major
states for a new balance of power.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.220
Great Britain went on to become one of the
strongest states.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:19.350
And years later, Queen Victoria and her husband
Albert came up with a plan to maintain that

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:23.260
political power: They married their children
to monarchs across Europe.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:25.100
And at that time, you know,

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:31.440
all royal marriages
were fundamentally about dynastic unions, about

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.990
cementing political allegiances, about building
new political alliances.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.510
It started with their daughter Vicky, the
eldest of nine children.

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:45.100
She married the heir to the Prussian throne,
the largest and most powerful of the German states.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.180
Albert’s vision had always been, and Victoria shared it,

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.340
that Prussia of all the German

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:57.830
states was the one that would end up leading
the way towards a great unified Germany.

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:04.810
They wanted to build strong connections with
Germany and see them as being a force for

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:09.119
good and constitutional benign 
monarchy across Europe.

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:14.320
Their children Alice, Beatrice, Helena, Leopold,
and Arthur also married German royalty.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.950
Their eldest son, Prince Albert Edward married
a Danish princess whose brother was King of

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.900
Greece; two more important European states.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.970
But when their son Alfred wanted to marry
the daughter of the Russian Tsar, things became

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:26.960
a bit more complicated:

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:32.640
There was a long history about
Queen Victoria's deep, deep apprehensions about

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.940
Russia, for any of her children marrying into
Russia.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.420
Well, the Russian monarchy was an autocracy,

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:42.920
whereas the British monarchy as such was
a constitutional monarchy.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:46.780
There was a whole long period of Russophobia
in Britain.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:51.940
The two states were also extremely competitive
over territory in Central Asia and Eastern Europe,

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.280
where they fought a bloody war in the
1850s.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:58.840
But the marriage was allowed and by the 1880s
Queen Victoria’s children were in several

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:01.390
important branches of Europe’s monarchies.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:03.660
But did that bring peace to Europe?

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:04.460
Not quite.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.820
See, Germany did unify in 1871, but it wasn't peaceful.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:12.940
Prussia fought a series of bloody wars and
consolidated the other German states.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:17.960
In Russia, the royal Romanov family was losing
its grip on power.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:22.420
Members of the monarchy were being hunted
and the Tsar was assassinated in 1881.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:27.080
The royal unions didn't play out as Queen
Victoria planned, but she continued to make

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:28.530
more matches anyway.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.660
She had 42 grandchildren in total,

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.920
and these 7 ended
up on royal thrones.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:39.220
The eldest, Wilhelm II, who was already in
line to be the next Emperor of Germany, married

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:41.360
a German princess in 1881.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.180
The hope was that he would steer a unified
and powerful Germany

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:45.900
into an alliance with Great Britain.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:49.530
George was in line to be the King of Great
Britain and married a minor British royal

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:50.709
family member.

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:54.990
Alexandra married Nicholas, who was related
to George and Wilhelm, and both became the

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:56.820
Tsar and Tsarina of Russia.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.260
And four more granddaughters married European
royalty, fulfilling Victoria’s vision.

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:05.459
I mean, when you look at Queen Victoria by
the end of her life

00:04:05.459 --> 00:04:07.620
she really was the grandmother of Europe.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:12.140
Take for example this family photo, where
Queen Victoria is with her daughter and grandson,

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:13.600
the rulers of Germany.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:17.500
Her son, Britain’s next king, and her granddaughters, the future

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:20.520
Tsarina of Russia and future Queen of Romania.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:25.240
Here’s the soon-to-be King of England and
his look-alike cousin the soon-to-be Tsar

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:25.940
of Russia.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.860
And here's some of the children and grandchildren
together.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:35.540
Finally, this is King Edward of Great
Britain and his nephew Kaiser Wilhelm of Germany

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:38.600
at Queen Victoria’s funeral in 1901.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:42.990
After her death, the family ties that Queen
Victoria had strung around Europe, would not

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:46.440
bring peace, but the most destructive war
Europe had ever seen.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:53.160
The Kaiser, the king of Gothenberg,

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.280
make ready to sweep the field.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.360
The Tsar of Russia mobilizes.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.480
England joins the battle royale.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:05.139
World War One broke out in 1914 and split
this family apart.

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:09.630
Wilhelm’s Germany along with Austria-Hungary
and the Ottoman Empire, fought an alliance

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:11.880
led by Britain, Russia and France.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.460
These countries were neutral.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.960
Say Victoria had lived 'til we were on the
brink of war.

00:05:19.979 --> 00:05:21.880
I think it would have broken her,

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.520
totally broken
her heart,

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:26.889
to know that her grandchildren ended

00:05:26.889 --> 00:05:28.760
up at war with each other.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:34.320
The war killed over 10 million people and
ended the era of monarchy in Europe.

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:38.240
Wilhelm, Sophia, and Marie were all forced
to abandon their thrones.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:42.700
Revolution swept through Russia and Alexandria
and Nicholas were executed by Communists.

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:47.640
The British monarchy survived, but the war
forced them to rethink their political strategy.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:53.680
George, King George V, and his wife
Queen Mary were very very astute.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:06:00.180
They saw that the monarchy had to be more
people friendly, had to be more accessible,

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:03.600
not just sitting there in great robes in glory.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.770
You know, with their crowns on. Had to be much

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:11.560
more out on the street, hands-on, meet the people,
win their confidence.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:14.220
The kind of monarchy we now have with Queen Elizabeth.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:18.360
That approach not only helped modernize British
monarchy over the last century,

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:19.140
but it also

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.840
changed the face of royal weddings, forever.

